WEBVTT

00:00.180 --> 00:01.350
Instructor: All right, so once again

00:01.350 --> 00:03.030
it's time for you to get a bit of practice

00:03.030 --> 00:04.740
with what we've been talking about.

00:04.740 --> 00:08.160
This time it's an exercise on merging and conflicts.

00:08.160 --> 00:10.170
Now this exercise is a bit different,

00:10.170 --> 00:11.940
it takes a different form.

00:11.940 --> 00:15.510
Instead of just following a step-by-step set of instructions

00:15.510 --> 00:18.570
where I could have asked you to make this file,

00:18.570 --> 00:21.870
make this branch, merge that branch into this branch

00:21.870 --> 00:23.640
and then fix the conflict.

00:23.640 --> 00:25.950
I could tell you exactly the steps you need to do

00:25.950 --> 00:29.490
to create a merge conflict and then resolve it.

00:29.490 --> 00:33.240
Instead of that, it's gonna be kind of the opposite.

00:33.240 --> 00:36.660
I'm gonna tell you that I want specific requirements

00:36.660 --> 00:37.493
for our merge.

00:37.493 --> 00:40.890
For example, I want you to create a scenario

00:40.890 --> 00:42.810
that results in a fast forward merge.

00:42.810 --> 00:44.850
So I'm not gonna tell you the name of the file

00:44.850 --> 00:48.300
what to change, what to add, what to commit, what to merge.

00:48.300 --> 00:50.580
All that I have are these three scenarios

00:50.580 --> 00:52.860
and then you need to engineer them.

00:52.860 --> 00:56.010
And while it may not be as easy to follow

00:56.010 --> 00:58.800
or as mindless in a way where you just step by step

00:58.800 --> 01:00.540
do what the instructions say,

01:00.540 --> 01:02.100
it's definitely a better test

01:02.100 --> 01:05.190
of your understanding of how merging works.

01:05.190 --> 01:07.470
So you'll need to make a new repo,

01:07.470 --> 01:09.930
make a file or two in the repo of your choice.

01:09.930 --> 01:11.010
Name it whatever you want.

01:11.010 --> 01:14.250
It doesn't matter, just someplace you'll be working in.

01:14.250 --> 01:17.550
So I'll call mine greetings.txt.

01:17.550 --> 01:20.910
So I recommend you just stop right now and try it.

01:20.910 --> 01:23.070
The instructions are pretty straightforward.

01:23.070 --> 01:26.490
Basically, it will say something like, make a new branch,

01:26.490 --> 01:28.890
do some work in the repo, it's very vague.

01:28.890 --> 01:30.630
Do some work in the repo such that

01:30.630 --> 01:32.850
when you merge that new branch into master

01:32.850 --> 01:35.700
it generates a fast forward merge.

01:35.700 --> 01:36.690
So what does that mean?

01:36.690 --> 01:38.430
Where do you do that new work?

01:38.430 --> 01:39.660
What does it look like?

01:39.660 --> 01:41.430
It doesn't matter what it actually looks like,

01:41.430 --> 01:42.930
the contents of a commit,

01:42.930 --> 01:45.600
but just generate a fast forward merge.

01:45.600 --> 01:47.070
Same thing here.

01:47.070 --> 01:48.090
Create a new branch.

01:48.090 --> 01:49.590
Make some changes to the repo

01:49.590 --> 01:52.290
such that when you merge the new branch into master

01:52.290 --> 01:54.210
it results in a merge commit.

01:54.210 --> 01:56.400
So again, very vague

01:56.400 --> 01:59.160
and this should not result in any merge conflict.

01:59.160 --> 02:02.280
So you have to understand or you have to try and think about

02:02.280 --> 02:05.640
how do I create a conflict and how do I avoid a conflict?

02:05.640 --> 02:08.760
And then the final piece is to do the same thing,

02:08.760 --> 02:11.190
work on a new branch, make some changes

02:11.190 --> 02:15.420
to that branch or to master or both, who knows, being vague,

02:15.420 --> 02:17.010
such that when you merge the new branch

02:17.010 --> 02:20.190
into the master branch, it results in a merge conflict.

02:20.190 --> 02:22.410
So merge it, see if you're right

02:22.410 --> 02:26.038
and then resolve the, hey, there we are,

02:26.038 --> 02:28.080
I don't know why that would didn't load all the way,

02:28.080 --> 02:29.613
resolve the conflict.

02:30.660 --> 02:32.100
So I'll try this,

02:32.100 --> 02:33.540
but I recommend you do it on your own.

02:33.540 --> 02:35.520
This is a very important exercise.

02:35.520 --> 02:37.800
Merging is important to understand the distinction

02:37.800 --> 02:39.660
between these types of merges.

02:39.660 --> 02:41.560
So I'm gonna make a new folder

02:42.630 --> 02:45.573
make directory merging exercise,

02:46.500 --> 02:51.330
I'll CD into that, make sure I'm not in a repo, I'll init.

02:51.330 --> 02:54.240
And then I'm just gonna add this file, I'll be working with

02:54.240 --> 02:58.053
I'll call it greetings.txt and I'll add and commit.

03:00.330 --> 03:02.820
So this is on the master branch by default,

03:02.820 --> 03:05.643
add greetings file.

03:07.200 --> 03:10.020
So the first bit is generate a fast forward merge.

03:10.020 --> 03:12.780
So make a new branch and do some work on that branch

03:12.780 --> 03:15.750
or somewhere, so that when I merge it into master

03:15.750 --> 03:18.030
we get a fast forward merge.

03:18.030 --> 03:19.953
So I can name this branch anything.

03:20.850 --> 03:23.220
I'm gonna go with Spanish,

03:23.220 --> 03:26.610
because I'm doing these greetings, different languages,

03:26.610 --> 03:28.740
I'll have like Hola and Bonjour

03:28.740 --> 03:29.913
and hello and stuff.

03:30.930 --> 03:32.640
So I make this new branch

03:32.640 --> 03:34.920
and to generate a fast forward merge.

03:34.920 --> 03:37.050
Again, I hope you're pausing and doing it yourself,

03:37.050 --> 03:38.820
but if you need a reminder,

03:38.820 --> 03:40.020
all that needs to happen is that

03:40.020 --> 03:42.510
when we branch off of master like we have

03:42.510 --> 03:44.760
we don't touch master again.

03:44.760 --> 03:47.400
We can add as many commits as we want to Spanish

03:47.400 --> 03:49.320
and then we merge that into master,

03:49.320 --> 03:51.960
but we don't change the master branch in the meantime.

03:51.960 --> 03:54.630
So all that master has to do is catch up on these commits

03:54.630 --> 03:56.520
that we've added to Spanish.

03:56.520 --> 03:59.130
So if I open my files,

03:59.130 --> 04:02.160
so open, I'll just open everything in this folder.

04:02.160 --> 04:06.720
Here it is, I'll add in Hola in Spanish.

04:06.720 --> 04:09.480
So that's our greeting and we'll add this.

04:09.480 --> 04:10.770
I'm just gonna do a single commit

04:10.770 --> 04:12.960
that will give me a fast forward merge.

04:12.960 --> 04:17.283
So git commit -m, add Spanish greeting.

04:20.340 --> 04:22.530
Now I'm on the Spanish branch.

04:22.530 --> 04:25.410
The goal is to merge the branch into master.

04:25.410 --> 04:26.973
So I need to switch to master.

04:28.320 --> 04:30.930
And then, I'm gonna generate that fast forward merge

04:30.930 --> 04:32.820
by git merging Spanish.

04:32.820 --> 04:36.120
So git merge the Spanish branch, whatever branch you did.

04:36.120 --> 04:38.520
When we merge it in there we are

04:38.520 --> 04:40.560
I got a fast forward merge.

04:40.560 --> 04:42.810
So if you see that whatever your contents are,

04:42.810 --> 04:43.950
whatever your file name is,

04:43.950 --> 04:45.850
if you saw a fast forward, good to go.

04:46.920 --> 04:47.940
So again, what happened is

04:47.940 --> 04:50.550
that I had some changes on a branch, some new commits

04:50.550 --> 04:51.870
just one commit in my example,

04:51.870 --> 04:54.960
but it could have been 10 commits or more, doesn't matter.

04:54.960 --> 04:58.680
But what matters is that my master branch that I merged into

04:58.680 --> 05:02.340
has not changed or did not change in the meantime.

05:02.340 --> 05:04.830
So all I had to do was catch up fast forward

05:04.830 --> 05:06.990
to get those new commits.

05:06.990 --> 05:08.880
Now that's gonna change with part 2.

05:08.880 --> 05:11.430
Generate a merge commit with no conflicts.

05:11.430 --> 05:13.590
So this time I'm gonna make a new branch

05:13.590 --> 05:15.480
and make some commits on it.

05:15.480 --> 05:17.760
But to generate a merge commit,

05:17.760 --> 05:21.330
I'm also going to make a commit on the master branch.

05:21.330 --> 05:25.260
And to make my life easier to avoid conflicts

05:25.260 --> 05:26.700
git can sometimes merge stuff

05:26.700 --> 05:29.310
in the same file without conflicts.

05:29.310 --> 05:32.700
But what I'm going to do is just make a new file on a branch

05:32.700 --> 05:35.190
and we know for sure there won't be a conflict.

05:35.190 --> 05:37.440
So let me do this full screen here.

05:37.440 --> 05:40.923
I'm gonna make a branch git switch -c French.

05:42.360 --> 05:45.930
And then, on this branch I'm gonna add a new commit.

05:45.930 --> 05:47.130
I'm gonna do some work.

05:47.130 --> 05:48.720
So why don't I just add

05:48.720 --> 05:50.853
to my greetings file, Bonjour.

05:53.075 --> 05:57.120
Bonjour, nope, okay?

05:57.120 --> 05:58.350
And then, I'll add that change.

05:58.350 --> 06:00.540
So git add greetings,

06:00.540 --> 06:04.350
git commit -m add French greeting.

06:04.350 --> 06:08.250
So now this branch has a commit that master does not.

06:08.250 --> 06:10.050
I'm not gonna merge just yet.

06:10.050 --> 06:12.450
If I did, it would be a fast forward merge.

06:12.450 --> 06:14.370
So now I'm back on the master branch

06:14.370 --> 06:16.053
and I'm gonna make a new file.

06:17.190 --> 06:18.100
What do you call

06:20.790 --> 06:22.563
goodbye and au revoir?

06:23.520 --> 06:25.050
Are those greetings?

06:25.050 --> 06:26.430
Is there a term for that?

06:26.430 --> 06:30.633
Ah, how about farewells.txt?

06:32.160 --> 06:34.140
So I'll make this farewells file

06:34.140 --> 06:36.480
and it's just gonna say goodbye.

06:36.480 --> 06:38.430
So now I have some work.

06:38.430 --> 06:42.000
If I add and commit on the master branch, a new commit

06:42.000 --> 06:44.520
git add farewells,

06:44.520 --> 06:48.900
git commit -m add farewells file.

06:48.900 --> 06:50.010
That's on the master branch.

06:50.010 --> 06:51.570
So now there's a commit on the master branch

06:51.570 --> 06:53.250
that doesn't exist on the French branch,

06:53.250 --> 06:54.990
and there's a commit on the French branch

06:54.990 --> 06:56.910
that doesn't exist on the master branch,

06:56.910 --> 06:58.620
but there shouldn't be conflicts,

06:58.620 --> 07:01.440
because those commits don't overlap.

07:01.440 --> 07:03.510
This commit was on the farewells file,

07:03.510 --> 07:04.740
totally separate file.

07:04.740 --> 07:06.840
The other commit on the French branch

07:06.840 --> 07:08.550
was in the greetings file.

07:08.550 --> 07:09.990
But even if they were in the same file

07:09.990 --> 07:12.450
it doesn't necessarily mean there would be a conflict,

07:12.450 --> 07:13.860
but we know for sure there won't be

07:13.860 --> 07:15.390
or at least there shouldn't be.

07:15.390 --> 07:18.060
So make sure no uncommitted changes

07:18.060 --> 07:20.073
and now git merge French.

07:21.180 --> 07:23.190
And we get this merge commit.

07:23.190 --> 07:26.070
I'll just leave it as is merge branch French,

07:26.070 --> 07:28.380
close outta there and there we go.

07:28.380 --> 07:32.250
If I type git log, we now have that new commit in there.

07:32.250 --> 07:33.300
Let's do it one line.

07:34.710 --> 07:37.530
So here is where the French branch was,

07:37.530 --> 07:39.240
the master branch was here,

07:39.240 --> 07:40.680
and now we have a new merge commit,

07:40.680 --> 07:43.680
and this is where the master branch currently points to.

07:43.680 --> 07:45.780
Now the final piece is to do the same thing,

07:45.780 --> 07:48.030
but now deliberately generate a conflict

07:48.030 --> 07:49.350
and merge that conflict.

07:49.350 --> 07:51.753
Well, not merge it, resolve the conflict.

07:52.590 --> 07:55.530
So I'll make a third branch.

07:55.530 --> 07:58.830
What do I want to change on one of those branches?

07:58.830 --> 08:02.040
So I'm gonna change the same file, the greetings file.

08:02.040 --> 08:04.950
On the master branch, I'll probably add hello in there

08:04.950 --> 08:06.210
and then I'll make another branch

08:06.210 --> 08:08.100
where I can add a different greeting.

08:08.100 --> 08:11.340
How about Japanese?

08:11.340 --> 08:13.500
If I can spell konnichiwa correctly,

08:13.500 --> 08:17.430
we'll put that on the greetings file on the Japanese branch.

08:17.430 --> 08:18.600
So I'm gonna make a branch right now

08:18.600 --> 08:22.593
git switch -c Japanese.

08:24.060 --> 08:27.300
So on this branch I'll put konnichiwa in there

08:27.300 --> 08:28.893
and then I will add and commit,

08:30.210 --> 08:32.940
git status, git add greetings,

08:32.940 --> 08:36.423
git commit -m add Japanese greeting.

08:37.440 --> 08:39.100
Now I'll switch back to master

08:41.040 --> 08:42.810
and before I do any sort of merging,

08:42.810 --> 08:45.330
I'm also going to make a change to greetings

08:45.330 --> 08:46.890
and this will generate a conflict,

08:46.890 --> 08:49.140
especially because it's on the last line.

08:49.140 --> 08:52.590
And I just updated the last line on the Japanese branch.

08:52.590 --> 08:53.973
So I'll put hello in here.

08:55.020 --> 08:58.650
So now if I add this, git add greetings,

08:58.650 --> 09:03.273
I'm on the master branch, git commit -m, add hello.

09:04.200 --> 09:05.850
Now we're going to have a conflict.

09:05.850 --> 09:08.610
When I merge that Japanese branch in,

09:08.610 --> 09:11.580
it has a commit that updates this line

09:11.580 --> 09:13.680
and there's a commit on this branch

09:13.680 --> 09:15.450
that the Japanese branch does not have,

09:15.450 --> 09:17.400
that also updates this line.

09:17.400 --> 09:21.783
So we should get a conflict, git merge Japanese.

09:23.970 --> 09:25.800
And we do, that's good,

09:25.800 --> 09:28.710
because that's what the exercise called for, conflict.

09:28.710 --> 09:30.480
So let's merge it.

09:30.480 --> 09:33.840
I believe I just said resolve it in any way you'd like.

09:33.840 --> 09:35.640
So I'm gonna keep both of them.

09:35.640 --> 09:36.473
Why not?

09:36.473 --> 09:39.600
So I'll do accept both changes if I use VS code,

09:39.600 --> 09:40.590
I'll just click that

09:40.590 --> 09:43.563
or I can just delete these symbols myself.

09:44.430 --> 09:46.500
Up to me how I wanna handle that.

09:46.500 --> 09:49.140
You don't have to keep both of them, but I will.

09:49.140 --> 09:52.080
So we save and that's not enough to resolve it.

09:52.080 --> 09:54.510
I mean, technically I guess you could say

09:54.510 --> 09:56.190
we've resolved it in the file,

09:56.190 --> 09:59.220
but if I type git status,

09:59.220 --> 10:01.800
it tells me that we have unmerged paths.

10:01.800 --> 10:03.990
And if I do a git log,

10:03.990 --> 10:06.210
we don't actually have a merge commit yet.

10:06.210 --> 10:08.100
So I need to add those changes.

10:08.100 --> 10:10.380
Remember, after we resolve conflicts,

10:10.380 --> 10:14.190
we add the files with the changes and then we commit

10:14.190 --> 10:17.100
and we'll do something like resolve conflicts for now

10:17.100 --> 10:21.540
or merge X branch, and now we are done.

10:21.540 --> 10:23.643
So if we do our git log one more time,

10:26.040 --> 10:28.530
we now see we have this new merge commit

10:28.530 --> 10:31.320
where we resolved the conflicts ourself manually,

10:31.320 --> 10:33.810
git couldn't just make the commit for us

10:33.810 --> 10:36.180
and we successfully met those requirements.

10:36.180 --> 10:38.910
So first off, we made a fast forward merge.

10:38.910 --> 10:40.380
We did some commits on a branch,

10:40.380 --> 10:42.840
we merged 'em into a branch that had not changed,

10:42.840 --> 10:45.780
no new commits on that branch, so it just caught up.

10:45.780 --> 10:47.970
Then we had two different merge commits.

10:47.970 --> 10:50.790
One, where we tried to not have conflict.

10:50.790 --> 10:53.790
So I just edited two different files, one on each branch,

10:53.790 --> 10:56.850
and then one where we deliberately tried to get conflicts,

10:56.850 --> 10:58.590
so I edited the same file,

10:58.590 --> 11:00.510
two commits on different branches,

11:00.510 --> 11:03.180
same line, there was a conflict, we resolved it

11:03.180 --> 11:05.580
and then we added and committed and we are done.
